# Oak Production Code Standards

Write production-ready code only. Do **not** add tests, test files, test scripts, or test-only refactors unless the user explicitly asks.

## Architecture (monorepo)

Packages: `backend/`, `ai-backend/`, `ui-board/`, `extension/`, `shared/`.

- Keep package boundaries clear: relay I/O in `backend`, AI/prompt/schema in `ai-backend`, visualization/UX in `ui-board`, page DOM/runtime in `extension`.
- Put cross-package contracts in `shared/` (types, event names, default ports/URLs, shared constants). Do not redefine the same `DomNode` / payload types in multiple packages.
- Prefer small modules with one job over growing god-files (e.g. `App.tsx`). Extract helpers, prompts, schemas, formatters, and protocol clients into named modules.

## No hardcoding

Never scatter magic values. Configure once, import everywhere.

| Kind | Do | Don't |
|------|----|--------|
| Ports / base URLs | Env (`PORT`, `AI_PORT`, `VITE_*`) + shared defaults | Duplicate `http://localhost:3847` / `3848` in each app |
| Secrets | `process.env` only (`OPENAI_API_KEY`, etc.) | Inline keys, tokens, credentials |
| Model / profile paths | Env with a single named default (`OPENAI_MODEL`, `PROFILE_FILE_PATH`) | Repeat model ids / file names across packages |
| Socket / message names | Shared constant maps (like extension `MSG`) | String literals copied across files |
| Limits / timeouts | Named constants near the module that owns them | Inline timeouts/size caps copied ad hoc |
| Domain data | Load from files/config (`profile.md`) | Embed long candidate data in code |

```ts
// ❌ BAD — duplicated literals
const DEFAULT_SERVER = 'http://localhost:3847';

// ✅ GOOD — shared config + env override
import { DEFAULT_BACKEND_URL } from '../../shared/config';
const serverUrl = import.meta.env.VITE_BACKEND_URL || DEFAULT_BACKEND_URL;
```

## Modular, systemized structure

- **Single source of truth**: one definition for types, defaults, and event names.
- **Pure helpers**: formatting and tree transforms stay side-effect free and importable (follow `tree-export.ts`).
- **Thin edges**: Express routes and React components orchestrate; they should not own protocol details.
- **Named constants at top of owning module**; export when reused. Prefer `as const` maps for protocol enums.
- **Fail clearly**: validate required input early with actionable errors.
- **Match existing style**: TypeScript in UI/extension, ESM in backends; reuse local patterns before inventing new ones.

## Production quality bar

- Handle error paths with status/message useful to the caller; no empty `catch`.
- Keep functions focused; if a block needs a comment to explain "what section this is", extract a function/module.
- Avoid drive-by refactors unrelated to the task, but when touching duplicated constants/types, consolidate into `shared/` or a package-local config module.
- Do not add markdown docs or tests unless requested.
